  "details": [],
  "plan": {
    "title": "Memory: learn which questions are the right ones, from what reviews already record",
    "problem": "Every plan starts from nothing. The records say a lot about what you care about, but no plan reads\nthem. You took the recommended option on every question you answered from a list, and wrote your\nown words on 3 of 16: the options were too abstract (D-022), the question was framed wrong (D-023),\nor you accepted with a condition (D-003). You accepted 85 of 91 of the agent's calls. The steps you\nrewound are the ones you later flagged or questioned, and one was rewound again after its revision.\nMost of your own-words notes are questions. And some things got past a review and were changed\nlater: a decision superseded (D-056 by D-063), a call accepted and reversed after review (the\nfile tools, k3), a part reworked within days. Each of those is a question that should have been\nasked, or a call that should have stopped.\n\nThe aim is the right questions, not fewer. The tool can also improve itself, which needs a\nyardstick outside its own numbers: it could \"improve\" by asking less and counting that as success.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "n": 1,
        "title": "Every review records its reviewer and the skill version",
        "text": "*Independent.*\n\nIntake adds the reviewer (git's `user.email` where the review is recorded, or the page's viewer) and\nthe reelplanning version that built the video to every review in `reviews/`. Old reviews are left as\nthey are. Without the version, a change to the skill can't be told apart from a change in the plans;\nwithout the reviewer, one person's taste can't be told apart from what works."
      },
      {
        "n": 2,
        "title": "The repo's memory: `reel status` says what reviews show",
        "text": "*Needs step 1 (to tell reviewers apart; works without it for one reviewer).*\n\n`reel status` ends with at most five lines, worked out each time from the ledger and `reviews/`:\nhow often a recommendation was taken; the questions answered in own words, and why; the steps\nrewound, and those rewound again after a revision; the quick checks answered wrong; the misses (step\n4). Each line has an id, and `reel memory <id>` prints its evidence: the reviews, the words, the\ntimes. The skill says to read it before writing a plan and when tagging calls. Nothing is stored\nthat the reviews do not already hold."
      },
      {
        "n": 3,
        "title": "Your memory, across repos",
        "text": "*Needs step 1.*\n\nWhen a review is recorded, a short summary of it (verdict, own-words answers and their reasons,\nrewinds, wrong quick checks, the kinds of call flagged) is appended to a file of your own, outside\nany repo (question 1). `reel memory --you` works out the same few lines across every repo you have\nreviewed in: your taste, not this repo's facts. The skill reads both."
      },
      {
        "n": 4,
        "title": "Misses decide what stops",
        "text": "*Needs step 2.*\n\nA miss is something a later plan, fix or system-video review changed that an earlier review let\nthrough: a decision superseded, an accepted call later flagged or reversed, a part reworked soon\nafter. `reel memory` traces each to the question or call that passed it, and its kind (a call's\ntags, a question's topic). A call of that kind stops the walkthrough again, whatever its streak of\naccepts (D-084's rule gains this one exception), and the next plan that touches that part asks that\nkind of question."
      },
      {
        "n": 5,
        "title": "A retro you start, checked against a fixed benchmark",
        "text": "*Needs steps 2–4.*\n\n`reel status` suggests a retro when one is due (question 2). `reel retro` starts a plan: skill edits\nthe evidence supports, each citing it, and it must cut at least as many words as it adds. It is\nreviewed like any plan. Before it is accepted, the benchmark (the Bob Dylan example, redone from the\nsame prompt, next to its text and HTML baselines) is run with the old and the new skill and\ncompared, so the retro is judged by something outside the loop's own numbers."
      }
    ]
  },
